\title{Exact Equations}
\date{2021-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\transclude{DE1M}

\p{If #{P(x, y)\,dx + Q(x, y)\,dy} is exact, use the multivariate chain rule:}

##{df = \underbrace{\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}}_{P}dx + \underbrace{\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}}_{Q}dy,}

\p{so there exists #{f(x, y)} such that #{\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} = P} and #{\frac{\partial f}{\partial y} = Q}: solve these to determine #{f}. Partial derivatives commute, so #{\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\big( \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \big) = \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}} and #{\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\big( \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} \big) = \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}} are equal. A \em{necessary} (but not sufficient) condition for exactness:}

##{\text{ODE exact} \implies \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} = \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}.}

\transclude{DE1N}

\transclude{DE1O}

\transclude{DE1P}

\transclude{DE1Q}
